WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.462
A drug molecule is often imagined occupying a protein

00:00:04.462 --> 00:00:08.028
pocket. Another possibility is that it persuades two

00:00:08.028 --> 00:00:09.365
proteins to meet.

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:19.342
For years drug action was pictured as occupancy.

00:00:19.342 --> 00:00:23.234
Induced proximity instead asks whether a molecule can

00:00:23.234 --> 00:00:25.667
bring two biological partners together.

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:35.875
A bifunctional agent can link a target protein to an E3

00:00:35.875 --> 00:00:38.833
ligase and initiate ubiquitination, redirecting the

00:00:38.833 --> 00:00:40.806
cell's own disposal machinery.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:49.835
The two proteins must form a productive complex. Linker

00:00:49.835 --> 00:00:53.245
length, orientation and cooperativity matter in ways

00:00:53.245 --> 00:00:56.169
simple binary affinity does not describe.

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:05.405
A bridge that dissociates too fast fails to act, while

00:01:05.405 --> 00:01:08.965
one that never releases may fail to turn over.

00:01:08.965 --> 00:01:10.547
Residence time becomes functional.

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:20.898
Induced proximity makes an event the pharmacological

00:01:20.898 --> 00:01:24.839
objective. Its success depends on complexes that form

00:01:24.839 --> 00:01:27.795
and the selectivity of their biology.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:36.175
Induced proximity can also relocalise or stabilize a

00:01:36.175 --> 00:01:40.365
protein, revealing that the real modality is the event

00:01:40.365 --> 00:01:41.762
the molecule causes.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:51.338
What matters is the cellular event a molecule produces,

00:01:51.338 --> 00:01:54.361
and whether exposure sustains it where needed.
